1
00:00:01,020 --> 00:00:02,009
Welcome back.

2
00:00:02,430 --> 00:00:06,210
In this lesson, we are talking about gerunds and infinitives.

3
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:10,170
What are gerunds and infinitives?

4
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:14,500
So a gerund is a verb with -ing ending.

5
00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:23,340
For example, playing and an infinitive is the verb with the preposition to, for example, to play.

6
00:00:23,670 --> 00:00:26,610
Or it can be without the preposition.

7
00:00:26,610 --> 00:00:27,840
So just play.

8
00:00:29,460 --> 00:00:34,200
Now, what is the problem actually with gerunds and infinitives?

9
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:44,580
Well, the problem is when we have a sentence, for example, like this one, I enjoy watch movies based

10
00:00:44,580 --> 00:00:45,690
on true stories.

11
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:50,040
Now, in this sentence, we have two verbs in the row.

12
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:52,260
We have enjoy and watch.

13
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:57,720
We know what to do is enjoy because this is our main verb in the sentence.

14
00:00:57,720 --> 00:01:00,600
So we just take the tense that we need.

15
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:06,030
For example, I enjoy generally it means I'm speaking in present simple.

16
00:01:06,030 --> 00:01:07,430
So I say enjoy.

17
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:13,200
If I spoke about the past, I would say I enjoyed.

18
00:01:13,620 --> 00:01:19,590
So we know what to do with the first verb, but sometimes we don't know what to do with the second verb.

19
00:01:20,010 --> 00:01:25,470
And for the second verb we have three options.

20
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:32,190
And those options are to infinitive, which would be to watch.

21
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:36,300
The second option is to use gerund.

22
00:01:37,140 --> 00:01:38,970
Which would be watching.

23
00:01:39,780 --> 00:01:45,300
And the third option to use infinitive without to, just watch.

24
00:01:46,750 --> 00:01:51,250
And the million dollar question is, which one to choose?

25
00:01:52,480 --> 00:01:54,650
The first question is which one to choose.

26
00:01:54,670 --> 00:01:57,790
And the second question is, how do I know?

27
00:01:57,820 --> 00:02:04,540
How do I decide which of those three options I need to choose in my sentence?

28
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:10,150
Now, the answer to this question is surprisingly easy.

29
00:02:10,419 --> 00:02:15,670
There is no special difficult rule to memorize here.

30
00:02:16,360 --> 00:02:24,640
The thing that we need to look at is actually the first verb, this first verb here.

31
00:02:25,030 --> 00:02:26,890
In this case, it's enjoy.

32
00:02:28,030 --> 00:02:35,500
This first verb in the sentence is the boss, and it decides which form we need to choose for the second

33
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:36,010
verb.

34
00:02:37,060 --> 00:02:38,230
Now, how does it work?

35
00:02:39,010 --> 00:02:46,950
Well, here on this slide, you see a bunch of verbs, after which we need to use gerund.

36
00:02:46,960 --> 00:02:51,670
So the second verb is in gerund after these verbs.

37
00:02:51,670 --> 00:02:55,210
So let's just go and look at them one by one.

38
00:02:56,710 --> 00:03:10,960
Admit, avoid, allow, celebrate, consider, deny, dislike, discuss, enjoy, fancy, feel like

39
00:03:11,320 --> 00:03:28,210
finish, hate, like, love, mind, miss, practice, prefer, recommend, resume, spend time, stop, suggest,

40
00:03:28,450 --> 00:03:33,730
give up, go on, keep, keep on and be worth.

41
00:03:34,690 --> 00:03:40,180
This is not the whole list, but these are the most commonly used verbs.

42
00:03:40,330 --> 00:03:49,420
And the main point is that you know that if you see these words, the next verb after this is going

43
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:50,620
to be in gerund.

44
00:03:50,830 --> 00:03:52,210
How does it work?

45
00:03:52,240 --> 00:03:54,040
Let's look at two examples.

46
00:03:54,940 --> 00:04:02,770
The first is If your phone got wet, I recommend switching it off until it dries.

47
00:04:03,940 --> 00:04:09,070
Now you can see that we have two verbs here - recommend and switching.

48
00:04:09,670 --> 00:04:13,060
And you remember that recommend is the boss.

49
00:04:13,150 --> 00:04:19,930
This verb decides which form do we need to use for the second verb

50
00:04:20,980 --> 00:04:24,970
So let's check. Recommend.

51
00:04:25,960 --> 00:04:28,780
Do we have recommend in this list here?

52
00:04:29,870 --> 00:04:30,290
Yes,

53
00:04:30,290 --> 00:04:32,600
we do, recommend this right here.

54
00:04:33,760 --> 00:04:42,100
That's why we know that often recommend we use the next verb switch with -ing, so recommend switching.

55
00:04:43,180 --> 00:04:49,600
The second example Project managers spend a lot of time talking to project stakeholders.

56
00:04:50,020 --> 00:04:51,820
Again, spend time.

57
00:04:52,870 --> 00:05:02,170
When you say spend a lot of time, there should be a thought in your head Aha, this verb to spend is

58
00:05:02,170 --> 00:05:06,550
in this list is in this bunch, to spend time.

59
00:05:06,970 --> 00:05:11,740
So, you know after this you need to use the gerund form.

60
00:05:11,860 --> 00:05:14,410
So spend a lot of time talking.

61
00:05:16,630 --> 00:05:18,760
Now let's look at to infinitive.

62
00:05:19,390 --> 00:05:23,320
We use to infinitive for the second verb.

63
00:05:23,350 --> 00:05:25,960
After this bunch of verbs.

64
00:05:26,650 --> 00:05:27,790
Let's look at them.

65
00:05:28,300 --> 00:05:37,120
Here we have: want, need, learn, can afford, choose, agree, arrange,

66
00:05:37,210 --> 00:05:39,130
ask, decide,

67
00:05:39,190 --> 00:05:48,430
deserve, expect, fail, forget, help, hesitate, hope, offer,

68
00:05:48,820 --> 00:06:00,760
plan, pretend, promise, refuse, remember, request, seem, threaten, volunteer, would like and

69
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:01,810
can't wait.

70
00:06:02,740 --> 00:06:07,420
Again, this is not the complete list, but these are the most common

71
00:06:07,420 --> 00:06:08,940
and they will help you a lot.

72
00:06:09,750 --> 00:06:12,320
Now let's look at the examples.

73
00:06:13,580 --> 00:06:16,580
I want to learn another framework this year.

74
00:06:18,380 --> 00:06:20,180
Want is the boss.

75
00:06:20,180 --> 00:06:24,050
It decides which form is going to be here.

76
00:06:24,350 --> 00:06:29,100
And we know that want is in this list - to want, to need.

77
00:06:29,120 --> 00:06:30,830
These are very, very common.

78
00:06:32,330 --> 00:06:39,710
So I think it goes automatic that you say, I want to do something and this is because

79
00:06:40,610 --> 00:06:41,960
want is here.

80
00:06:43,020 --> 00:06:45,930
So I want to learn. Another example.

81
00:06:45,930 --> 00:06:51,360
If you need more information, don't hesitate to call or leave me a message.

82
00:06:51,780 --> 00:06:53,430
Again, hesitate.

83
00:06:53,460 --> 00:06:56,460
You can find it in here in this list.

84
00:06:56,700 --> 00:07:02,070
So you know, after this verb you use to infinitive, so to call.

85
00:07:03,040 --> 00:07:03,520
Okay.

86
00:07:04,420 --> 00:07:10,210
And the last choice, the last option that we have is infinitive without to.

87
00:07:10,390 --> 00:07:13,510
We use it with all modal verbs.

88
00:07:13,520 --> 00:07:20,440
So any time you have a modal verb, and I think you know it, you use the verb without the to.

89
00:07:21,280 --> 00:07:28,750
And modal verbs are can, could, may, might, shall, should, will, would, must.

90
00:07:28,780 --> 00:07:35,080
These are all modal verbs and another two verbs, and they are make and let.

91
00:07:35,440 --> 00:07:42,970
After those verbs we use infinitive without to. The examples.

92
00:07:43,450 --> 00:07:46,900
He couldn't take this job because he wasn't ready yet.

93
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:49,300
He couldn't take.

94
00:07:49,300 --> 00:07:52,000
We know could is modal.

95
00:07:52,120 --> 00:07:55,900
So after that we don't use to, we don't use -ing.

96
00:07:55,930 --> 00:08:01,150
We just take the bare infinitive, infinitive without to.

97
00:08:02,170 --> 00:08:05,920
If you're stuck, you should try googling your issue first.

98
00:08:06,730 --> 00:08:18,640
Again, should is a modal, so should try. Now this until here was very easy, simple and clear,

99
00:08:18,640 --> 00:08:27,250
I think. The only thing that you need to do here is you just have to go back to those slides, gerund

100
00:08:27,250 --> 00:08:36,580
and infinitive, and you have to try to memorize this list of verbs, to memorize them to know that

101
00:08:36,580 --> 00:08:39,280
after them you use to infinitive.

102
00:08:39,520 --> 00:08:46,330
And it's good practice to memorize them with the second verb, for example, you take the verb play

103
00:08:46,570 --> 00:08:52,360
and you take each of those verbs from here and make the phrase with them.

104
00:08:52,360 --> 00:08:59,530
For example, want to play, need to play, learn to play, can't afford to play, choose to play and

105
00:08:59,530 --> 00:09:00,040
so on.

106
00:09:00,040 --> 00:09:06,880
So try to play with those words, make up sentences with them, make up phrases with them, and that

107
00:09:06,880 --> 00:09:10,720
will help you memorize them easily.

108
00:09:11,990 --> 00:09:12,290
Okay.

109
00:09:12,290 --> 00:09:18,050
Now, this here was good and the fun stuff begins on the next slide.

110
00:09:19,400 --> 00:09:28,700
So there are verbs that can be followed by a gerund or the infinitive, but the meaning changes.

111
00:09:29,780 --> 00:09:32,060
Now this here becomes interesting.

112
00:09:32,840 --> 00:09:34,580
Let's look at those examples.

113
00:09:35,150 --> 00:09:39,650
She stopped smoking versus She stopped to smoke.

114
00:09:41,490 --> 00:09:51,000
We use the same words, only the form is different, which is gerund or infinitive, and the two sentences

115
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,700
have absolutely different meanings.

116
00:09:54,270 --> 00:09:58,920
The first sentence she stopped smoking means she doesn't smoke anymore.

117
00:09:58,950 --> 00:10:00,210
She quit smoking.

118
00:10:00,390 --> 00:10:01,860
She doesn't do it anymore.

119
00:10:02,640 --> 00:10:04,350
And she stopped

120
00:10:04,350 --> 00:10:08,130
to smoke means that she was walking.

121
00:10:08,820 --> 00:10:11,820
Then she stopped and she smoked.

122
00:10:13,440 --> 00:10:17,160
So she stopped in order to smoke.

123
00:10:18,750 --> 00:10:21,270
So these sentences are very, very different.

124
00:10:21,270 --> 00:10:26,430
And what makes them different is either we use gerund or we use the infinitive.

125
00:10:27,570 --> 00:10:34,080
Another example with stop She stopped coding because she went on to do project management.

126
00:10:34,350 --> 00:10:39,000
It means she doesn't code anymore, because she does another job.

127
00:10:39,660 --> 00:10:46,980
And the second is She was reading when she had this idea about her application, she stopped to code

128
00:10:46,980 --> 00:10:48,180
it right away.

129
00:10:48,630 --> 00:10:53,070
It means she stopped whatever she was doing, reading in this case.

130
00:10:53,790 --> 00:10:58,500
She stopped this and she started coding.

131
00:10:58,650 --> 00:11:02,220
So she stopped in order to do something else, to code.

132
00:11:03,750 --> 00:11:04,170
Good.

133
00:11:04,560 --> 00:11:07,710
So I think you get the idea here.

134
00:11:07,740 --> 00:11:14,910
Now, let's look at other verbs that have the same, that have the same behavior.

135
00:11:15,840 --> 00:11:20,430
For example, we have forget, forget. On one side

136
00:11:20,820 --> 00:11:23,370
we can use it with the two infinite.

137
00:11:23,370 --> 00:11:26,760
And we say, I forgot to call him.

138
00:11:27,510 --> 00:11:33,690
In this case, it means I didn't call him because I forgot to do it.

139
00:11:33,930 --> 00:11:35,430
I forgot to call him.

140
00:11:36,750 --> 00:11:37,550
That's easy.

141
00:11:37,560 --> 00:11:46,260
And on the other side, we have I forgot calling him and this sentence means I don't have the memory

142
00:11:46,260 --> 00:11:47,280
of calling him.

143
00:11:47,730 --> 00:11:54,780
It means basically it means I forgot that I called him or I forgot if I called him.

144
00:11:56,150 --> 00:11:58,520
I don't have this memory of calling him.

145
00:12:00,650 --> 00:12:02,510
Go on, to go on.

146
00:12:03,770 --> 00:12:05,720
On one side we have

147
00:12:06,110 --> 00:12:11,510
After getting certified in scrum, he went on to learn Kanban.

148
00:12:12,450 --> 00:12:13,590
What does it mean?

149
00:12:14,070 --> 00:12:17,970
He went on to learn Kanban.

150
00:12:18,480 --> 00:12:24,000
It means he finished one task and moved to another task.

151
00:12:24,030 --> 00:12:29,910
It means he finished learning Scrum and he went on to learn Kanban.

152
00:12:29,940 --> 00:12:33,840
So one task - finished, the second task.

153
00:12:35,490 --> 00:12:37,830
And on the other side we have

154
00:12:37,980 --> 00:12:43,680
He went on learning MS Project until he felt confident using it at work.

155
00:12:44,610 --> 00:12:49,710
And this here means went on the learning, went on doing something

156
00:12:49,800 --> 00:12:53,850
It means he continued learning MS Project.

157
00:12:53,850 --> 00:13:01,050
So to go on in this case means to continue, to continue doing the same task.

158
00:13:03,450 --> 00:13:06,600
And another pair - to quit.

159
00:13:06,780 --> 00:13:12,150
Again, on one hand He quit to find a more interesting project.

160
00:13:13,430 --> 00:13:14,240
That's understood.

161
00:13:14,690 --> 00:13:18,860
You quit this job in order to work somewhere else.

162
00:13:20,620 --> 00:13:21,640
On the other side

163
00:13:21,640 --> 00:13:26,830
we have He quit looking for a new job when he got a pay raise.

164
00:13:27,490 --> 00:13:28,050
All right.

165
00:13:28,950 --> 00:13:30,120
That's understood as well.

166
00:13:30,570 --> 00:13:34,350
So it means he doesn't look for a new job anymore.

167
00:13:34,380 --> 00:13:43,040
He only wanted to quit because the salary was bad, so he was looking for another job.

168
00:13:43,050 --> 00:13:45,450
So he was in the process of looking.

169
00:13:45,540 --> 00:13:51,090
And when he got a pay raise, he quit looking at means he stopped doing it.

170
00:13:51,090 --> 00:13:52,710
He doesn't do it anymore.

171
00:13:54,450 --> 00:13:56,880
And the last one here is try.

172
00:13:57,880 --> 00:13:59,170
One side we have

173
00:13:59,470 --> 00:14:03,760
I tried to run my executable, but it gave me an error.

174
00:14:04,600 --> 00:14:06,970
It means I attempted this action.

175
00:14:06,970 --> 00:14:10,870
I attempted to run it, but didn't succeed.

176
00:14:11,740 --> 00:14:16,150
And on the other side, we have I don't have VPN access.

177
00:14:16,390 --> 00:14:20,170
I tried restarting VPN client, but it didn't help.

178
00:14:20,350 --> 00:14:21,430
So I tried

179
00:14:21,430 --> 00:14:28,300
restarting means restarting was basically one of the options that I checked, that I tried, maybe I

180
00:14:28,300 --> 00:14:29,350
tried something else.

181
00:14:29,350 --> 00:14:31,150
So I tried doing this.

182
00:14:31,150 --> 00:14:32,440
I try doing that.

183
00:14:32,710 --> 00:14:36,040
So this was one of the options that I tried.

184
00:14:37,800 --> 00:14:38,250
Good.

185
00:14:39,120 --> 00:14:48,540
And another point here is that there are verbs that can be followed, again, either by gerund or by an

186
00:14:48,540 --> 00:14:49,410
infinitive.

187
00:14:49,890 --> 00:14:54,120
And there is little change or basically no change in meaning.

188
00:14:54,120 --> 00:14:57,940
So we can use either gerund or infinitive.

189
00:14:57,960 --> 00:14:59,460
It basically doesn't matter.

190
00:14:59,940 --> 00:15:05,910
And good examples of those verbs are like, love, hate,

191
00:15:05,910 --> 00:15:08,940
prefer, start, begin and continue.

192
00:15:09,240 --> 00:15:14,070
For example, it started to rain or it started raining.

193
00:15:14,700 --> 00:15:20,370
No change in meaning, doesn't change the sentence in any way.

194
00:15:21,690 --> 00:15:25,860
I love creating mind maps and I love to create mind maps.

195
00:15:25,950 --> 00:15:29,180
Again, the meaning is the same or almost the same.

196
00:15:29,190 --> 00:15:35,160
So basically we don't care which form we use in this case.

197
00:15:36,680 --> 00:15:37,570
Okay good.

198
00:15:37,580 --> 00:15:39,620
Now that was it at this point.

